Connect ProChat Workbench to One Repository

**Status:** draft requiring manual review before recording or publication  
**Product lane:** ProChat Workbench  
**Bounded outcome:** connect one local repository and inspect one exact file without changing anything

Today I’m going to connect ProChat Workbench to one real local repository.

And by the end of the video, we will prove one thing.

Workbench can identify the correct source and read one exact file without modifying the project.

No broad scan.
No hidden edit.
No commit.

Just one bounded first task.

My name is Steve.

I spent more than 12 years testing software in real environments.

That work taught me to separate what I assume from what the evidence actually proves.

So before asking ChatGPT to change a project, I want to know three things.

Which source is selected.
Which path is being read.
And whether the worktree changed.

Let’s start with the result.

Here is the repository source selected in Workbench.

Here is the exact file we asked it to read.

And here is the final Git status.

The repository remains unchanged.

That is the complete outcome for this video.

Now let’s reproduce it calmly.

First, I open ChatGPT with ProChat Workbench available.

Workbench is the local project layer.

ChatGPT remains the reasoning interface.

The repository remains on this computer.

Before doing anything, I ask Workbench for its available sources.

I do not guess the repository name.

I verify it from the returned source list.

For this demonstration, I select one sanitized repository prepared for recording.

The repository contains no client information.

It contains no secrets.

It contains no unrelated private work.

Now I lock that source for the task.

This matters because the same file path could exist in more than one project.

A path without a verified source is incomplete context.

Next, I check the current Git status.

This is our before-state evidence.

I can see whether files are modified, deleted, untracked, or staged before Workbench reads anything.

For this first demonstration, the repository is clean.

That makes the result easy to verify.

Now I ask one bounded question.

Read the repository README and tell me the stated project purpose.

Do not modify any file.

Workbench reads that exact path.

ChatGPT summarizes what the current file says.

The file is the source of truth for this answer.

The summary is an interpretation of that source.

Those are not the same thing.

If the summary sounds plausible but does not match the file, the file wins.

Now I verify the exact path that was read.

I do not treat a general project summary as evidence that the whole repository was inspected.

This demonstration proves only that one source was selected and one file was read.

It does not prove that Workbench understands every part of the project.

It does not prove that the repository is correct.

And it does not prove that any future change will be safe automatically.

Now I run Git status again.

The before-state and after-state match.

No file changed.

Nothing is staged.

Nothing is committed.

That visible result completes the task.

The control point is simple.

Verify the source before trusting the context.

Then keep the first task small enough to inspect completely.

For a later change, we will add more controls.

Exact approved paths.
Diff review.
Targeted validation.
And explicit Git decisions.

But not today.

Today we proved one bounded starting point.

One repository.
One exact file.
No modification.
Visible Git evidence.

The limitation is equally important.

A successful read does not authorize a write.

Every change still needs its own scope, review, and validation.

The next action is simple.

Review ProChat Workbench on GitHub.

Then try one read-only task on a repository you are allowed to use.

Before recording or publishing this script, the source-selection flow, repository example, product behavior, and all visible screen content must be reviewed manually.
